  "tips": {
    "greetings": "💡 \"Merhaba\" works everywhere. \"Teşekkür ederim\" is appreciated — locals love when tourists try Turkish.",
    "food": "💡 \"Kebap\" is not just shish — explore lahmacun, pide, and meze. Tea (çay) is offered everywhere; accepting is polite.",
    "directions": "💡 Istanbul has metro, tram, and ferry. The Istanbulkart (transit card) works on all — buy one at the airport.",
    "numbers": "💡 Currency is Turkish Lira (₺ / TRY). Carry cash for bazaars. Exchange rates vary — avoid airport desks.",
    "emergency": "💡 Dial 112 (general), 155 (police), 112 (ambulance). Tourist police in Istanbul: (0212) 527 4503.",
    "shopping": "In the bazaar, agree the price before anyone wraps the goods.",
    "medical": "Call 112. Clinics in Istanbul are used to travelers.",
    "immigration": "Have your passport and the e-visa if you needed one, plus the hotel address.",
    "hotel": "Ask for the Wi-Fi, and whether the hammam costs extra.",
    "transport": "Use BiTaksi or a ferry. Buy an Istanbulkart for trams and buses."
  },
  "pronGuide": {
    "title": "Turkish Sounds",
    "tips": [
      "Turkish is phonetically regular — every letter is pronounced the same way every time",
      "Ş sounds like \"sh\", Ç like \"ch\", Ğ (soft G) silently lengthens the preceding vowel",
      "The undotted ı (without a dot) is a unique central vowel — like the \"u\" in \"circus\"",
      "Ü sounds like the French \"u\" or German \"ü\" — round your lips and say \"ee\""
    ]
  },
